<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baba Farid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1179–1266</span> · Also known as Fariduddin Ganjshakar, Farid al-Din Masud Ganjshakar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baba Farid, Fariduddin Masud Ganjshakar, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> master of the Chishti order in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, who lived and taught at Ajodhan, now Pakpattan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, where his shrine remains a major place of pilgrimage. He was one of the first poets to write in Punjabi, and verses attributed to him are included in the Guru Granth Sahib, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> scripture. The town of Faridkot traditionally takes its name from him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baba-farid.jpg" alt="Baba Farid" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Sheikh_Fareed_Shakarganj,_ca.1823%E2%80%9324_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
